# ASTRA-SC1 cycle 2 — spell-check / accent-detection re-review

**FAIL for using the current L1 corrected-label overlays in wave 1c.** Seven of the fourteen cycle-1 findings are fixed, three are partly fixed, and four are honestly deferred. The important SF1 repairs hold. However, the new release consumer can turn explicitly unknown or uncertain evidence into accepted corrections, and its edit guard permits removal of marks outside the supported output vocabulary. Both PRESENCE and FULL are affected. Keep the present overlays as experimental proposals until the release conditions below are met.

Reviewed 17 October 2026. This is a read-only review, not an implementation or an authorization to launch, stop, or rerun a job.

## Identity, scope and evidence

References prefixed **M:** resolve under `/Users/foivoskarounos-zamparloukos/Projects/greek-ocr-toolkit-spellcheck`, branch `opus/spellcheck`, HEAD **`fabcd6d44b61528f3d343f0f1f1368d9f7129e0d`**. References prefixed **L:** resolve under `/Users/foivoskarounos-zamparloukos/Projects/greek-ocr-toolkit-spellcheck-l1`, branch `opus/spellcheck-l1`, HEAD **`6b91ebaa29c7f7b2a7da55910af75a9a140de56b`**. Line numbers refer to those checkouts. The user identifies the latter as the code running in Clariden job **3679313**; I did not contact Clariden. The pre-submit wording and older launch commit in `L:docs/labelfix/spellcheck/clariden/L1_REPORT.md:3–7` are historical handoff information, not a current job-status observation.

I reran the original cycle-1 probe **unchanged**, by copying it into scratch because it writes beside itself. I ran **128 targeted main-worktree tests and 11 L1 tests, all passing**, plus synthetic composition/geometry probes and saved-artifact readbacks. Tests used 11 distinct native fixture crops; I additionally viewed six existing L1 mock crops, for **17 distinct native crops**, below the 30-crop cap. No network, CTC or neural forward pass, GPU work, training, full-cohort validator, or bulk crop processing was run. Corpus totals in this review are saved-artifact readbacks, not fresh measurements. Neither repository was modified.

Evidence is in [ASTRA-SC1c2_evidence](ASTRA-SC1c2_evidence/). Principal files are `review_probes_original.py`, `review_probe_results.json`, `pytest_main.log`, `pytest_l1.log`, `new_probes.py`, `new_probes_main.json`, `new_probes_l1.json`, `geometry_probes.py/json`, `agreement_probe.py/json`, `cn1_unheld_coverage.json`, `m2_line_gate_readback.json`, and `local_receipt_checks.json`.

## Check 1 — every cycle-1 finding

“FIXED” closes the original defect at its stated boundary; it does not certify population accuracy. “ACCEPTED-AS-DEFERRED” accepts the disclosed plan, counts as **not fixed**, and does not authorize a new consumer to treat missing qualification as established safety.

| Cycle-1 finding | Verdict | Current evidence and remaining boundary |
|---|---|---|
| **B01 — orthographic deletion in print output** | **FIXED** | `M:greekocr/labelfix/reading.py:267–296` preserves the input proposals and emits flags/HOLD; normalization is explicit. The unchanged epsilon-circumflex probe now preserves `ε͂` with no removal. C1-after-consonant/C3 holdbacks remain at `:54–70`. SF1 correctly accounts for **100 held-out deletions including 10 unknown**, plus **40 DEV including 3 unknown**, rather than adding the unknowns twice (`M:docs/labelfix/spellcheck/SF1_REPORT.md:9–26`). This fixes K1 deletion; it does not protect unsupported original-label marks from the new L1 renderer, C2-M01 below. |
| **M01 — truth provenance, correlated supervision, untouched books** | **ACCEPTED-AS-DEFERRED** | Agreement-derived labels remain in `M:scripts/labelfix/clariden/r1_common.py:89–97`; adjudication/agreement-proxy selection remains in `M:greekocr/labelfix/linemetric.py:16–28`. SF1 explicitly leaves this open (`SF1_REPORT.md:117–119`). L1 explicitly says the fresh R0 set is **not drawn** and must exclude its 318-document inventory (`L:L1_REPORT.md:38`, under `docs/labelfix/spellcheck/clariden/`). No locked fresh-book result or complete per-slot provenance is claimed. L1 therefore remains a pseudo-label experiment, not independently verified print truth. |
| **M02 — fused/unmapped truth coverage** | **ACCEPTED-AS-DEFERRED** | The frozen mapping remains in `M:scripts/labelfix/clariden/r1_score.py:56–105,117–134`. Probe readback still has **715 unmapped held-out predictions**, 10,614 mapped, 10,046 mapped with known truth; the frozen fused cohort has **16 known / 330 unknown** and only **5 positive semantic marks**. Arm A raw counts differ by denominator: **3818/33/235** on all frozen components versus **3818/33/43** on scored components. Improved reporting is real, but character-indexed independent fused/unmapped evaluation is still R0 work. L1 cannot inherit a broad fused-accuracy qualification from these scores. |
| **M03 — unsupported truth becomes known empty** | **FIXED** | `M:greekocr/labelfix/markshape.py:114–133` and `M:scripts/labelfix/s6_validate.py:45–71` preserve unsupported truth as `None` with a reason. The unchanged double-accent probe now returns `{0: None}`. SF1 reports **zero existing truths changed** in its saved replay and four synthetic witnesses, without inventing prevalence (`SF1_REPORT.md:28–39`). Evaluation truth is fixed; the separate L1 output-vocabulary problem remains. |
| **M04 — clipping substitutes foreign pixels** | **FIXED** | Blobs carry exact membership and parent IDs (`M:greekocr/labelfix/ink.py:70–100,199–231`); clipping intersects that mask (`:464–479`), and deduplication conserves its union (`M:greekocr/labelfix/reading.py:330–360`). The original witness is now **32 → 19 original pixels**, not 48 foreign pixels. Exact-mask and serialization tests pass. The old probe’s `result_parent_ids` inspects the bounding rectangle, so its `[0,1,2]` is not a membership test; the exact-mask checks are the relevant custody evidence. |
| **M05 — incomplete union case features** | **FIXED** | `M:greekocr/labelfix/segmentation.py:14–26`, `M:greekocr/labelfix/ink.py:119–123`, and `M:greekocr/labelfix/case.py:21–37` use complete union membership. The two-parent occupancy probe now returns **2/3**, not 1/3; the attached-mark union tests also pass. This establishes mask consistency, not a new independently measured capital-recognition gain. |
| **M06 — physical certification exceeds geometry** | **PARTLY** | Mask custody is repaired and S1–S3 adapters expose more state. Default segmentation still makes CTC-conditioned partitions and sets `segmentation_uncertain` without necessarily setting `localisation_unknown` (`M:greekocr/labelfix/segmentation.py:64–85`); `read_ink` still calls `certified_body` (`M:greekocr/labelfix/reading.py:323–325`). Default straight/scalar geometry remains (`M:greekocr/labelfix/case.py:94–154`). No geometry substitution qualified. S7 also has the adapter gaps in C2-M03. L1’s failure to consume existing uncertainty is now a release blocker, C2-B01. |
| **M07 — owner pages do not reproduce the evaluated reader** | **PARTLY** | The accepted S4 asset default, required configuration, asset/source/policy checks and common serialized page record are substantial fixes (`M:scripts/labelfix/owner_reading.py:13–42,76–110`; `M:scripts/labelfix/owner_pages_build_combined.py:189–206`). Reference-field invariance tests pass. But the combined builder has its own composition policy (`:158–177`), unlike M2/L1: it loses opening quotes and skips missing predictions. The hash faithfully identifies that different behavior; it does not establish production-policy parity. See C2-M02. |
| **M08 — owner/regression instruments can pass wrong states** | **PARTLY** | Global box matching, missing lines, legal ownership zones and old/new namespaces are fixed (`M:scripts/labelfix/box_regression.py:32–94`). The original order probe gives **0 lost / 0 added / 2 moved in either order**. Approved-unknown-name removal now fails; absent `no_top` targets are unresolved (`M:scripts/labelfix/s6_notes.py:64–100`). `owner_implicit_changes.py:14–23,30–75` removes neighbour widening and requires reviewed-page/sidecar binding. Remaining quote, self-boundary, fusion and matched-owner blind spots prevent full closure; C2-M02 and C2-N02 detail them. |
| **M09 — mislabeled baseline and incomplete score binding** | **FIXED** | Named/hash-checked baselines and prediction/extraction/checkpoint checks are present (`M:scripts/labelfix/clariden/r1_score.py:26–47,165–180`); separate raw/normalized outputs and receipts are at `:255–291`. Probe readback shows **S5 3714/249/339**, separately **S6 3731/245/322**. Reader support no longer borrows detector blobs (`M:greekocr/labelfix/reading.py:83–116`; scorer `:56–105`). Receipt tampering/current artifact tests pass. This repairs comparison identity and policy reporting; M01/M02 accuracy limitations remain. |
| **M10 — padding-sensitive windows and admission mistaken for observability** | **ACCEPTED-AS-DEFERRED** | The original probe still produces **32 versus 16 dark rows** after adding blank padding; full-height resizing remains (`M:scripts/labelfix/clariden/r1_extract.py:19–36`). A blank sample is still admitted, now honestly labeled `evidence_state=UNKNOWN`, `uncertainty=[no_pixel_evidence]` (`:80–83`). The window contract explicitly disclaims padding invariance/source validity. Preserving the trained input until R2 qualification is reasonable. **Ignoring the new UNKNOWN state when releasing corrections is not covered by that deferral**: C2-B01. |
| **N01 — historical validators inherit new defaults** | **FIXED** | S4/S5 abort before historical output writes (`M:scripts/labelfix/s4_validate.py:42–54`, `s5_validate.py:42–54`); S6 does likewise (`s6_validate.py:126–141`). Their dormant calls explicitly select raw behavior. All three guard tests and explicit-rendered-parts checks pass (`M:tests/labelfix/test_sf1.py:236–248`). Versioned SF1 replays retain superseded artifacts. |
| **N02 — sampling/loss changes and selected prevalence conflated** | **ACCEPTED-AS-DEFERRED** | The sampler/loss coupling remains; the rebuilt comparison discloses it and the selected agreement-pool prevalence (`M:scripts/labelfix/clariden/r1_score.py:255–279`; `SF1_REPORT.md:119`). No controlled arm ablation or deployment-prevalence estimate was added. L1 explicitly freezes A; that is a policy choice, not proof that A wins a pure sampling comparison. |
| **N03 — misleading fallback confidence** | **FIXED** | Missing case assets fail closed (`M:greekocr/labelfix/case.py:52–55`). Heuristic names carry `heuristic_unqualified`, `probability=None`, and a separate rejected-model probability (`M:greekocr/labelfix/markshape.py:80–111`); relevant tests pass. The diagnostic naming threshold is still 0.5 (`:51,75`) and no separately calibrated correction probability has been established. This verdict closes the attribution/fallback defect, not calibration. |

**Cycle-1 tally: 7 FIXED, 3 PARTLY, 4 ACCEPTED-AS-DEFERRED, 0 NOT FIXED; 7/14 fully fixed.** The four substitution recommendations are assessed separately below and are not additional cycle-1 findings in this denominator.

## Ranked findings from this re-review

### C2-B01 — BLOCKER: L1 accepts corrections despite recorded unknown or uncertain evidence

**Code path.** `L:scripts/labelfix/m2_run.py:122–142` indexes coverage but reads its reason only when the R1 prediction is missing. A present prediction bypasses `coverage.evidence_state=UNKNOWN` and `coverage.uncertainty`. The detector checks `localisation_unknown` and `unnamed`, but not `segmentation_uncertain` or directional observability. `:155–166` then emits ACCEPT/HOLD. `L:scripts/labelfix/clariden/l1_common.py:49–62` trusts those HOLD decisions and otherwise releases a skeleton-compatible proposal.

**Reproduction.** On both reviewed checkouts, the real M2 composer accepts detector-none/R1-none with each of: R1 `UNKNOWN/no_pixel_evidence`; R1 `UNKNOWN/out_of_image_span`; detector `segmentation_uncertain=True`; detector top/bottom clipping flags. Both modes return unheld `α`. In the L1 checkout, the real overlay function changes original `ά` to `α` with status **`corrected`**, in both PRESENCE and FULL. These are synthetic policy counterexamples, not measured production error rates (`new_probes_l1.json`).

This is not solely hypothetical state reachability. The saved mock keeps **four segmentation-uncertain slots on one line**, `592f0823e0984204631241d8-p000020-l0012`, starts 27, 28, 48, 49, in both 34-line kept sets. That line is unchanged, so it does **not** demonstrate a wrong corrected label. The same saved kept sets contain **262 clipping flags across 25/34 lines**. Those coarse edge flags can be neighbouring ink and are **not 262 demonstrated truncated marks**. No R1-UNKNOWN slot was found in the kept mock. The strongest evidence is the executable correction-gate counterexample, supported by actual propagation of segmentation uncertainty into kept output.

**Concrete fix.** Make the shared composer/release gate consume R1 uncertainty and detector unresolved ownership/segmentation. A prediction cannot turn absent/invalid observation into evidence of no mark. Preserve fused-but-resolved cases; do not ban all fused samples or restore a connected-component admission veto. Define relevant directional observability from validated crop/source masks; until then, route unresolved relevant clipping to review rather than silently certifying absence. Export these states with every overlay decision. Recompose/re-filter saved detector/window/R1 records under a new bound policy where sufficient evidence was retained; do not label old outputs repaired merely by changing the manifest. This safety gate does not require changing the trained R1 window or waiting for R2 retraining.

### C2-M01 — MAJOR: the overlay edit guard allows unsupported-mark and non-Greek-diacritic deletion

`L:greekocr/labelfix/reading.py:37–51` removes every original combining mark and reconstructs only the supported mark names. `L:scripts/labelfix/m2_run.py:133–144` only holds an unsupported letter if the detector emits a mark/unnamed mark. `L:scripts/labelfix/clariden/l1_common.py:29–30,56–62` compares skeletons after removing **all** combining marks; it therefore cannot reject edits outside the reader’s vocabulary. The line-level `greek_poly` gate does not establish per-character coverage.

The executable L1 probe releases **`ᾱ → α`**, **`é → e`** inside a row declared `greek_poly`, and **`ά̀ → α`**, as corrected with no HOLD in **both modes**. These demonstrate what the policy permits; their prevalence in the 124,669-line input is unmeasured. SF1’s M03 repair correctly calls such printed truth unsupported, but that state is not an output protection.

**Concrete fix.** Validate edits against explicit supported character/mark domains and multiplicities. Hold lines whose proposed edits erase unsupported source marks or unsupported-script diacritics unless a separate qualified reader covers them. An alternative is to preserve those characters unchanged with verified character correspondence; do not silently normalize them. Inspecting the original label to reject an unsupported edit is an output-scope guard, not feeding its mark name into pixel inference. Retain separate, explicit punctuation-edit policy.

### C2-M02 — MAJOR: the documented combined-builder defect remains, and CN1 is not L1-policy parity

`M:scripts/labelfix/owner_pages_build_combined.py:158–177` skips absent predictions/components, checks named-family disagreement, then calls `render_reading` without the opening-quote composition used by `M:scripts/labelfix/m2_run.py:148–150`. It also lacks M2’s explicit missing-R1, unlocalized and unnamed gates. `M:scripts/labelfix/cn1_measure.py:33–45` deliberately sets `reader_scored=True` for every system slot; its docstring discloses that this means output exists, not R1 coverage. That adapter cannot establish two-signal acceptance.

**Saved-output witnesses:** CN1’s `combined_boxes.json` loses the opening quote on `384c3f89-p000030-l0010` and `384c3f89-p000030-l0029`, while retaining their opening-quote evidence. The corresponding detector controls contain it. `M:scripts/labelfix/s6_notes.py:125` tests only whether `opening_quotes` is nonempty; the saved example-note records mark those quote/leading-sign clauses fixed (`M:docs/labelfix/spellcheck/CN1_checks.jsonl:59–60`), although the complete notes can still fail or HOLD for other reasons. The synthetic page probe reproduces `“α → α` with quote evidence still present.

**CN1 already discloses this correctly.** `M:docs/labelfix/spellcheck/CN1_REPORT.md:119–139` and `M:scripts/labelfix/cn1_report.py:65–76` distinguish physical punctuation evidence from displayed glyphs: two detected quotes disappear, three unsupported recogniser quote glyphs return, and a sixth line has only a spacing difference. The five glyph differences are outside the headline one combined-worse note. This review confirms a documented implementation defect; it is not a finding that CN1 hid those changes. “New” in the ranked counts means work/defects assessed since cycle 1, not necessarily first discovered here.

The saved CN1 combined records also have **eight R1-target slots with missing predictions but no HOLD** on `8b6b0de9-p000021-l0012`, **five unheld localization-unknown slots**, and **two unheld unnamed slots**. These are coverage/policy discrepancies, not fifteen independently established transcription errors; details are in `cn1_unheld_coverage.json`. CN1 explicitly documents detector fallback on the missing-R1 line (`CN1_REPORT.md:137–139`), so this does not invalidate its declared full-system denominator. It does mean its 87 HOLD slots describe that historical page policy, not M2/L1’s complete gate.

**Concrete fix.** Share one composer and serializer across the combined page, measurement and release paths. Keep detector-only/system-output coverage separate from R1-scored coverage and acceptance eligibility. Check emitted punctuation as well as retained physical evidence; add quote/no-prediction/unlocalized/unnamed parity witnesses. Preserve historical CN1, then regenerate a separately versioned result and page binding. The particular opening-quote loss is already avoided by M2/L1’s composer; do not attribute that page defect to the current L1 output.

### C2-M03 — MAJOR: S7’s adapters do not fully test the proposed physical contracts

This is a qualification defect in off-default adapters, not an assertion that S7 changed L1’s default detector.

**S2 drops candidates before its per-x clipping.** `M:greekocr/labelfix/ink.py:325–341,390–428` still selects detached candidates and slices attached marks using scalar centre-derived top/base values. The shared per-column arrays enter final clipping only at `:451–453`. My curved/sloped-guide probe has a detached **12-pixel mark whose 12 pixels are legal under their own columns**, yet `mark_evidence` returns **zero marks** because the scalar prefilter rejects it. A final per-x intersection cannot recover a candidate already discarded (`geometry_probes.json`).

**Provenance and mapping also fall short.** `M:greekocr/labelfix/toolkit_geometry.py:39–78` does create a shared guide object and retain toolkit baseline curvature, but collapses provenance to one string per guide, takes scalar height/median offsets, and does not carry per-x support/borrowing/local-scale evidence. Its isolated pseudo-page is not a real comparable-line page context; `text_lines(..., max_per_side=0)` omits neighbouring-row guides. At `:89–91` S1 selects maximum overlap without preserving an equal-best tie. Its line receipt can say ambiguous/count-disagreement/unassigned while the selected slot is resolved: the two-physical-box/one-alpha probe records `physical_mapping.ambiguous=True` but `localisation_unknown=False`, `segmentation_uncertain=False`, `ambiguity=None` (`:103–129`).

**Concrete fix.** Apply the same per-column guide masks to candidate generation, attached-body separation, side searches and final clipping; preserve exact source membership throughout. Carry guide support/fallback/borrowing at the locality at which it is used, and expose missing page context honestly. Preserve equal-best associations and propagate relevant unassigned/count ambiguity to affected slots rather than resolving it by list order. Qualify these corrected adapters against independent body/mark witnesses before treating their gains/losses as evidence about the toolkit approach. Neither counterexample estimates how much of S7’s aggregate regression it explains.

### C2-N01 — MINOR: `agreement_mode.py` is a divergent diagnostic, with an overwritten flag

At `M:scripts/labelfix/agreement_mode.py:28`, an external K1 flag sets `act='hold'`, but the later chain at `:35–39` overwrites it unless the flag also appears in the per-record state. At `:37`, PRESENCE/ZONE keep Google when R1 equals it even if the detector disagrees; M2 holds that disagreement. Executing the script’s actual decision loop on synthetic streams reproduced both behaviors (`agreement_probe.json`).

Make flags part of the decisive HOLD predicate and either call the shared policy or label this as a distinct historical counterfactual. Do not use its keep/HOLD counts as L1 acceptance counts. L1 calls M2 directly, so this script defect does not itself bypass L1’s K1 flags.

### C2-N02 — MINOR: box stability is not ownership or exact-pixel stability

The repaired global assignment is sound for its stated box-matching objective. But `M:scripts/labelfix/box_regression.py:59–73` only reports a matched box as moved below IoU 0.6; it never compares matched owners at high IoU. An identical box reassigned from display slot 1 to 2 produces **no lost/added/moved/reading change** in the probe. Changed source membership inside an unchanged rectangle is likewise outside this instrument. Its CLI also unwraps sidecars and accepts notes by line ID without enforcing reviewed-page/notes binding (`:102–107`). The stricter `owner_implicit_changes.py:58–75` checks page/sidecar association, but does not itself prove that an arbitrary supplied notes file was written against that page.

Add matched-owner, source-pixel and status diffs alongside box deltas; require an explicit notes-to-reviewed-snapshot binding for approval language. Preserve unresolved old-slot associations. Keep “0 box loss” as a geometric regression result, not a certificate of unchanged glyph ownership or owner approval. This is a remaining M08 limitation, not a failure of the repaired maximum-cardinality matching.

## Check 2 — what S7 implemented, and what its rejection establishes

The premise that all four recommended substitutions were implemented and rejected is incorrect. `M:docs/briefs/S7_toolkit_substitutions.md:17–27` and the runtime allowlist `M:greekocr/labelfix/reading.py:299–317` cover **S1, S2 and S3**. The original fourth package was **canonical straightening with inverse coordinates for R1/future alignment**, not the monotonic scope gate. It was not implemented or qualified here.

| Original recommendation | What was actually exercised | Assessment |
|---|---|---|
| **S1 — middle-region letter pieces, parent sets, unresolved fusion** | Calls `glyphs.letters.letter_boxes` on a glyph view; the toolkit restricts to the middle before grouping (`M:greekocr/glyphs/letters.py:52–54,70–110`) and retains unresolved physical splitting (`:123–145`). Adapter preserves parent IDs, piece counts and unresolved/shared groups (`toolkit_geometry.py:81–139`). | A meaningful, largely faithful use of the core API, not an import-only placeholder. CTC association remains a separate fallible adapter; tie/unassigned ambiguity is incompletely propagated, and reconstructed full-parent regions are not themselves proof of character ownership. **Keep this adapter off** given its regressions; do not reject the owner’s physical rule. |
| **S2 — one shared per-x guide system with provenance** | Preserves baseline curve plus an S6 body-height gauge correction, uses four-guide/font evidence, applies shared final clipping, and adds a geometric capital vote (`toolkit_geometry.py:39–78,142–162`). | **Partial implementation.** Scalar candidate filters, scalar local height and global provenance remain; a new capital heuristic changes another decision at the same time. C2-M03 proves an adapter failure. The reported capital/presence losses justify not shipping this candidate, not abandoning per-x guidance. |
| **S3 — source validity and directional cuts** | `ink.analyse` accepts masks (`M:greekocr/labelfix/ink.py:166–196`); `toolkit_geometry.py:165–197` consumes them and `skew_line` cut spans. The qualification loader calls `analyse(im)` without upstream validity masks (`M:scripts/labelfix/s7_validate.py:120–127`). | Real cut API use, but source-validity propagation was not demonstrated on the cached crop cohort. The broad ±0.9-x-height search envelope treats missing extent as UNSEEN even without edge ink. **Keep this broad policy off** for the current pipeline because it nearly eliminates output, while retaining observability as a necessary safety requirement. Its rejection does not make unseen pixels observed. |
| **S4 — straightened view plus inverse coordinates** | No corresponding adapter/qualification. | **NOT IMPLEMENTED**, not “rejected on qualification.” Keep it a future input-distribution experiment with explicit coordinate mapping and R2 qualification; never reuse cached CTC spans blindly after a crop/warp/scale change. |

S7’s report is more careful than “the toolkit failed”: `M:docs/labelfix/spellcheck/S7_REPORT.md:3–7` and its generator `M:scripts/labelfix/s7_report.py:23–31` explicitly reject these adapters/feature decisions. The qualification decisions are encoded in the report generator, supported by saved instruments; they are not a separate automated physical-truth oracle.

The negative adoption decisions are reasonable on those instruments. The launch control’s held-out presence P/R is **98.65% / 95.47%**, fused recall **60.49%**. S1 gives **98.10% / 94.63%**, fused recall **58.02%**, and total owner kind disagreements **89**: 76 of the original 80 remain plus 13 new. S2 gives **98.60% / 94.99%**, fused recall **60.49%**, with **88** disagreements: 73 remain plus 15 new. S3 leaves presence unchanged but flags **6,628 held-out slots UNSEEN** and holds **430/433** checked M2 lines. These are sufficient reasons not to replace the baseline with these candidates; they do not prove all geometry regressions were inherent to the toolkit.

### Qualification instruments and the “80 disagreements” target

The **80** are detector-versus-R1 **named-family disagreements**, not 80 independently verified geometry errors. The saved ledger correctly reports **67 known truth/proxy cases: R1 right 62, detector right 3, neither right 2; 13 unknown** (`S7_REPORT.md:143–153`). My readback also finds **33/80 have the same named above/below zone set**, while 47 differ. A same-zone disagreement can concern family/multiplicity; a different-zone disagreement can still arise from classification or association. Neither stratum alone diagnoses bad geometry.

Counting disappeared and newly introduced disagreements separately is useful. The report correctly avoids calling agreement a correction and keeps UNKNOWN unknown. But optimizing toward R1 agreement alone would reward correlated errors. Qualification needs independently adjudicated source pixels, owner/character association, observability, presence/multiplicity and exact name, with adjudication versus agreement proxy reported separately. A zero-geometry-change candidate cannot be expected to resolve S4’s shape-family mistakes.

The repaired box matcher and stricter owner clauses are improvements, but their limits matter here. S7’s below-meanline instrument compares pixels against the same generated S2 guide (`M:scripts/labelfix/s7_validate.py:135–145`); zero violations shows conformance to that guide, not that it follows printed bodies. `safe_zone`, `fusion`, and `whole_marks` clauses check own-boundary compliance, located count/nonoverlap and zone equality (`M:scripts/labelfix/s6_notes.py:101–129`), not full physical segmentation or exact naming. CN1 punctuation has the concrete false pass above. The complete-note counts are valuable regression constraints, not independent certification of every implied physical property.

For S3, requiring no loss of known corrections is a coverage objective, not a sufficient safety objective. Report accepted-edit error, unresolved/abstained fraction and retained coverage separately. First establish whether an UNSEEN decision is physically warranted; then narrow an overbroad observation envelope. Rejecting an over-abstaining adapter must not justify ignoring already recorded UNKNOWN states in the existing reader.

### What I would try next for detector presence evidence

Keep SF1’s exact-pixel custody and print-preserving defaults. Separate **owned physical mark evidence** from **S4 name/family predictions**: above/below/side source pixels, candidate multiplicity, body contamination and per-direction observation status should be inspectable before naming. A named-family decision can remain a diagnostic feature; it should not be described as an independent geometric measurement.

First repair the demonstrated per-x prefilter and association-state losses, then use a small, independently adjudicated development witness set stratified by the 80 disagreements, fused/unmapped characters, joined marks, capitals, curved lines and crop cuts. Preserve negative and unknown witnesses. Compare the baseline and corrected adapters on source-pixel ownership and false accepted edits as well as coverage. Reserve fresh R0 books for the eventual locked decision. For R1 input robustness, qualify baseline-aligned/source-validity-aware windows and coordinate adapters under R2; do not change the deployed trained input on the strength of these synthetic probes alone. Switching directly to ZONE would remove information and would not solve shared absence errors.

## Check 3 — K1, agreement modes, CN1, M2 and L1

### K1 and the meaning of PRESENCE

K1 now acts as a print-preserving warning/abstention layer. That is the correct B01 repair. It is not independent pixel evidence: its legality rules can flag a printed misprint, which should remain visible and be held for review.

**PRESENCE is a categorical mark-family agreement rule, not a pure “two geometric pixel signals agree” check.** M2 maps detector `parts` and R1 `parts` into families and compares those sets (`M:scripts/labelfix/m2_run.py:136–142`). Detector above-mark parts come from the frozen S4 whole/per-blob shape classifier and its fallback (`M:greekocr/labelfix/markshape.py:51–111`); geometry supplies subscript presence and some compatibility constraints. Holding the physical blob fixed while changing only its S4 name from acute to psili flips PRESENCE from accept to HOLD in my probe.

| Mode | Actual information compared | Valid interpretation |
|---|---|---|
| FULL | Exact named mark sets | Stricter agreement between two pipelines; still vulnerable to common omissions, common misclassification, unsupported output and bad observation. |
| PRESENCE | Breathing/accent/dialytika/subscript family sets; e.g. acute and grave share accent | A useful hypothesis for trading fine-name disagreement against coverage, with R1 choosing the final name. It still depends on S4 classification and does not verify that R1’s acute/grave/circumflex choice is printed. |
| ZONE | Legacy detector above/below booleans versus R1’s named zones in `agreement_mode.py:29–32` | A coarser diagnostic. It loses family/multiplicity information and is not the production M2/L1 mode. |

The two pipelines consume pixels differently, but share recogniser-derived bases/spans, and their evaluation/supervision has correlated agreement-proxy limitations. “Two pixel-derived pipelines agree” is accurate; “independently verified printed mark” is not. A calibrated acceptance/error claim needs the deferred independent evidence. FULL alone does not fix C2-B01 or C2-M01.

### CN1: useful clause accounting with explicitly limited output claims

`M:docs/labelfix/spellcheck/CN1_REPORT.md:9–17,33–57` distinguishes complete notes, overlapping facets and HOLD, and reports the combined current-note scores **26/49** and **25/57**, below strict detector **35/49** and **33/57**. The **87 held slots on 53/92 owner lines** are 80 kind disagreements plus 9 K1 flags with 2 overlapping. These are honest instrument counts, not 87 new print adjudications. Its refusal to call HOLD fixed is sound.

The unchanged punctuation/physical clause counts must be read as instrument outcomes, not preserved emitted text. CN1 explicitly makes that distinction and inventories the five rendered quote differences (`CN1_REPORT.md:119–139`); C2-M02 confirms the two omissions independently and identifies the policy-parity boundary. Likewise `reader_scored=True` means full-system output, as disclosed, rather than R1 coverage. CN1 is useful and candid historical regression evidence, but it does not validate the exact current L1 acceptance policy.

### M2: denominators and demonstrated limits

The inventory is **513 cards / 13 sources**, of which **473 Greek/mixed cards / 12 sources** are processed and **40 Russian cards** excluded. It is a selected diagnostic collection, not a random fresh-book test (`M:docs/labelfix/spellcheck/M2_REPORT.md:3–17`). Its **77** targets are literal mark-only error pairs; base-letter/punctuation-inseparable cases are excluded. The scorer’s pair-local matching/HOLD and correct-reference controls are explicit (`M:scripts/labelfix/m2_score.py:129–208,215–249`).

PRESENCE reports **25 corrected / 40 HOLD / 12 missed** target pairs; FULL **24 / 41 / 12**. Changes on previously correct control fragments are **30 versus 15**, not automatically 30 versus 15 proven new errors. Whole-line HOLD is **367/473 (77.6%)** versus **380/473 (80.3%)**. The report discloses **251 unmapped reference letters**, and its targeted 20-change visual audit reports **14 yes / 3 uncertain / 3 no**, including a polytonic printed circumflex changed to acute (`M2_REPORT.md:79,108–137`). That is direct evidence that agreement alone does not guarantee print correctness; the selected visual sample is not a precision estimate.

S7’s explicit monotonic exclusion reduces the checked set to **433**. Its **327 HOLD** is **69.13% of 473**, but **75.52% of the 433 checked cards**; both are disclosed (`S7_REPORT.md:183–198,218`). The unchanged 25/77 and fewer correct-control changes are a scope effect, not a geometry gain.

**M2’s pair correction count is not L1’s retained-line benefit.** A saved-data diagnostic applying L1-style whole-line no-HOLD and skeleton requirements to the original M2 outputs leaves 11 of PRESENCE’s 25 corrected pairs on no-HOLD lines, and **8** after the skeleton gate; FULL is 10 of 24, then **7**. These are still pair counts, not line counts, and the exercise is not an L1 training-population measurement (`m2_line_gate_readback.json`). It explains why 25/77 must not be used as expected wave-1c correction yield.

### L1 input, exclusions and runtime isolation

The scope is explicit and appropriate to the stated all-polytonic selection: **124,669 `greek_poly` rows / 318 documents** from 214,436 train rows. The six actual waves are round1 43,869; parliament 27,224; vouli 23,088; round2 17,026; mixed 13,227; openarchives 235. The extra 235 are disclosed rather than silently dropped. **89,767** other-script/monotonic rows are out of scope (`L:docs/labelfix/spellcheck/clariden/L1_REPORT.md:9–22`; `L:scripts/labelfix/clariden/l1_prepare.py:26–53`).

The saved receipt has zero intersections with the seven named exclusion sets; selection code canonicalizes identities and checks documents and lines, and planted-overlap tests pass (`L:l1_prepare.py:41–49`; `L:l1_run.py:54–62`, both under `scripts/labelfix/clariden/`). This is good exclusion plumbing. I did not rescan the full crop inventory. R0 must be outside these 318 documents **and** satisfy its broader upstream training/calibration exclusions; merely being absent from L1 is not enough to make a book fresh for every model.

The runtime setup copies E3 into **`venv_l1`**, rewrites paths only in the copy, installs **`scipy==1.17.1` with `--no-deps`**, and checks E3 before/after metadata and SciPy package hashes (`L:scripts/labelfix/clariden/l1_runtime.py:21–28,40–70,79–96`). The job prologue uses that venv and checks deployment/external manifests plus runtime verification (`L:scripts/labelfix/clariden/l1_job.sbatch:22–30`).

My local receipt readback confirms **259 policy source hashes and 2 asset hashes match**, and the policy digest matches the binding: `605b0721a06f9ebc552a466b150c0cc6497e932306cab29ae311e125550f5379`. Saved before/after E3 file-list digests are both `379466f022be1344d437e24d050ab54ea4ddad8009dc3d67aed2e84fb153af5b`, matching their files; the saved SciPy manifest also matches its receipt. This supports the recorded isolation procedure. The E3 lists bind paths/modes/sizes/mtimes/symlink targets, not every file’s contents, and this review did not independently inspect the live remote venv. There is no evidence here that E3 was modified.

### L1 parity gate and release format

The in-job gate is correctly **before training-crop alignment/detection** (`L:scripts/labelfix/clariden/l1_run.py:35–53`). It verifies 32 instrument crop hashes, executes detector/R1/full M2 composition, compares canonical detector/combined decisions and all HOLD reasons, and aborts on mismatch (`l1_verify.py:50–73`). Node runs cannot regenerate their own golden output (`l1_run.py:30–32`). This is a meaningful wrapper/runtime regression gate.

It establishes agreement with the frozen local implementation, including its policy defects; it does not establish correct printed labels. `l1_common.py:85–96` intentionally excludes probabilities and exact pixel masks from the projection. The parity inference uses batch size **64** (`l1_verify.py:58`), while production inference defaults to **512** (`l1_infer.py:49,75–80`); therefore the gate is not an exhaustive guarantee across production batch shapes or all crops. Qualify the actual production execution shape when updating the gate. The saved **32/32 exact** result is a mock/local receipt, not proof that job 3679313 passed its node gate.

The overlay/release design has several sound safeguards (`L:scripts/labelfix/clariden/l1_common.py:43–82,99–169`):

- Original source bytes are preserved; each overlay binds the original label hash, model/policy hashes, status and proposed label.
- Edit offsets are **original-label NFD offsets**. Evidence slot offsets are **recogniser NFD offsets**, explicitly separate. Consumers must honor that distinction.
- Any emitted HOLD rejects the **whole line**. Base/non-whitelisted-punctuation disagreement rejects the line, subject to the overbroad combining-mark exception in C2-M01.
- Duplicate/unknown runtime IDs and incomplete coverage fail; corrected/unchanged are the only kept statuses.
- Each corrected variant has a control containing the **exact same kept IDs and ORIGINAL labels**, and the written control payload is checked, not merely an in-memory alias.
- Manifests bind overlays, selections, kept IDs and sources; status coverage is reported per actual wave and source.

The saved mock has **5 corrected, 29 unchanged, 126 HOLD**, with **34 matched control IDs** in each variant. My readback verifies both overlay, kept-ID and control hashes. I viewed the five corrected native lines plus the retained segmentation-uncertain line. The crop views provide limited plausibility checks for particular mark proposals/punctuation normalization; they do not establish a correction precision, resolve every tiny mark, or cure the policy counterexamples.

`l1_run.py:74–81` writes a final complete run receipt only after release generation and source recheck. A timeout can leave shards or release files without completion. The local reviewed artifacts establish the mock and deployment preparation, not production completion. The actual job’s run receipt, node parity, complete shard/overlay hashes and counts must be read before claiming a production release exists.

The paired control is the right design for testing label changes **conditional on the same selected lines**. It does not by itself compare selection against the full original corpus or remove selection bias. Wave 1c must select exactly one bound variant, use its exact kept set for both arms, load corrected/unchanged labels from that release, and exclude HOLD/out-of-scope rather than falling back to original labels. A training consumer is not implemented or validated by the overlay writer alone.

## Conditions before L1 can be used by wave 1c

**Current answer: no.** Successful job completion or byte parity alone would not change this verdict.

1. Fix C2-B01 and C2-M01 in a versioned shared policy, preserve/reject unsupported edits explicitly, and regenerate both corrected and paired-control releases from adequate saved evidence. Bind the new code/policy and retain the original run as historical output.
2. Add the demonstrated UNKNOWN, uncertain segmentation, directional observability, unsupported-mark and non-Greek-diacritic witnesses to release tests. Ensure the page/measurement path uses the same acceptance semantics; do not substitute CN1’s old HOLD counts for this gate.
3. Revalidate the real production node/runtime and inference batch shape; require a completed, hash-consistent production run with exact selection, exclusions, overlay/kept/control correspondence and unchanged original source. This review supplies no current remote-success claim.
4. Before treating automatic edits as qualified labels, conduct an independent print audit of retained edits, including absence/deletion, fused/unmapped, clipping, rare/unsupported marks and correct-label controls. Keep calibration/development selection separate from the fresh locked R0 evaluation. If wave 1c is explicitly an experimental pseudo-label comparison, report that scope and its uncertainty; do not relabel it independently verified ground truth.
5. Verify the wave-1c consumer on the released artifacts: original-label hash checks, correct normalization/offset interpretation, exactly paired IDs, and no HOLD fallback or double application of changes.

The S7 adapters should remain off while repaired and requalified. None is a prerequisite for consuming already recorded uncertainty correctly. R0, R2 and the arm ablation remain legitimately deferred scientific work; those deferrals cannot supply a missing release-safety decision.

## Reproduction record

The targeted test commands were run from their respective repository roots with the requested interpreter, `PYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE=1`, the corresponding absolute `PYTHONPATH`, `-p no:cacheprovider`, and scratch `/tmp` pytest basetemps. Main tests were `tests/labelfix/test_sf1.py`, `test_s7.py`, `test_s4.py`, `test_s5.py`, `test_s6.py`, `test_k1.py`, and `test_r1.py`: **128 passed in 3.86 s**. L1 ran `tests/labelfix/test_l1.py`: **11 passed in 0.40 s**. These are targeted runs, not a claim to have repeated the full historical 223/235-test reports.

The unchanged original probe and the new probes write only inside `ASTRA-SC1c2_evidence`. `new_probes.py` takes the absolute repository root as its argument and was executed once with each checkout on `PYTHONPATH`. `geometry_probes.py` exercises real adapter/evidence functions with synthetic geometry; `agreement_probe.py` executes the diagnostic script’s actual decision loop with synthetic streams. Saved-data readbacks do not decode the full cohorts or rerun inference. Passing existing tests is compatible with the new counterexamples: they cover different contracts.

